\(\chi^{2}_{min}+1\). The error of \(dN/dy\) was then obtained by projecting this 3-D contour into \(dN/dy\) axis. Fig. 3.18 shows the plots of corrected non-photonic electrons spectra and the combining fit results for \(D^{0}\) and electrons spectra in \(d\) + Au collisions. The curve for \(p+p\) collisions is by scaling down that of \(d\) + Au collisions by \(\langle N_{bin}\rangle=7.5\pm 0.4\).

Once the \(dN/dy\) was extracted, the total charm production cross section per nucleon-nucleon interaction at RHIC energy can be calculated from Eq. 3.7:

\[\sigma^{NN}_{c\bar{c}}=\frac{d\sigma^{NN}_{c\bar{c}}}{dy}\Big{|}_{y=0}\times f =\frac{dN_{D^{0}}}{dy}|_{y=0}\times R\times\frac{\sigma^{pp}_{inel}}{\langle N _{bin}\rangle}\times f \tag{3.7}\]

In this equation, the factor \(R\) is the \(D^{0}\) fraction in total charm hadrons, as mentioned before, and \(f\) is a factor when extrapolating the \(dN/dy\) at mid-rapidity to full rapidity. This factor \(f\) was extracted from PYTHIA model study and we estimated a \(\sim 15\%\) systematic error on this factor from different parameters in that model. The charm production cross section per nucleon-nucleon collision at mid-rapidity and total cross

